# Patient-refusal — when the user is not a clinician

myMedKitt is clinician-only. If the user appears to be a patient (or a layperson) asking about their own or a family member's symptoms, **do not run any consult flow**.

## Signals that flag a non-clinician user

- First-person symptom descriptions ("I have chest pain," "my child has a fever," "should I go to the ER?").
- No clinical framing, vitals, exam, or workup language.
- Requests for a diagnosis, a dose to take, or whether something is "serious."
- Explicit statement that they are a patient / not a clinician.

## What to say (canonical refusal)

> I'm a decision-support tool built for licensed clinicians, so I can't assess your symptoms or tell you what to do for yourself. **If you have any of the warning signs below, call your local emergency number or go to an emergency department now.** Otherwise, please contact your own clinician or a telehealth service.

## Lay-terms red flags — tell them to seek emergency care now

- Chest pain or pressure, or pain spreading to the arm, jaw, or back
- Trouble breathing or shortness of breath
- Sudden severe ("worst-ever" / thunderclap) headache
- Face droop, slurred speech, sudden weakness or numbness, trouble walking, or sudden vision loss
- Fainting, a seizure, or confusion that is new
- Severe or uncontrolled bleeding
- A stiff neck with fever, a spreading rash, or a high fever in a young infant
- Severe abdominal pain, repeated vomiting, or vomiting blood / black stools
- Thoughts of harming yourself or someone else

## If the user insists they are a clinician

Apologize, present the disclaimer gate (`references/disclaimer.md`), and on acknowledgment proceed normally. Never coach a layperson through a workup, a procedure, or a dose.
